WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.030 --> 00:00:04.220
Ta-prohm isn't the most impressive ruin in
the world.

00:00:04.220 --> 00:00:07.620
It's not even the most impressive in the neighborhood.

00:00:07.620 --> 00:00:13.450
Angkor wat sits only a couple miles away -- it's
the largest religious monument ever built.

00:00:13.450 --> 00:00:21.999
But Ta-Prohm is a bit more human scale. And
it has merged with nature

00:00:21.999 --> 00:00:27.419
It's original name was Raja Vihara -- the
royal monastery -- and was dedicated to the

00:00:27.419 --> 00:00:29.119
Buddhist personification of wisdom.

00:00:29.119 --> 00:00:35.899
But it is a new place now, silk-cotton trees
and strangler figs have merged with the carved

00:00:35.899 --> 00:00:38.550
rocks to create new structures.

00:00:38.550 --> 00:00:44.870
The temple was built about 1000 years ago,
around 1186, just after Angkor wat, during

00:00:44.870 --> 00:00:49.280
the golden age of the Khmer empire in what's
now Cambodia.

00:00:49.280 --> 00:00:53.969
The Khmer controlled most of southeast Asia,
and the capital city Angkor was the biggest

00:00:53.969 --> 00:00:57.019
urban center in the world before the industrial
revolution.

00:00:57.019 --> 00:01:01.980
They had incredible technology to support
such a large congregation of humans for the

00:01:01.980 --> 00:01:02.249
time.

00:01:02.249 --> 00:01:07.430
Southeast Asia gets heavy rainfall in one
season, and less in another -- so the Khmer

00:01:07.430 --> 00:01:10.160
built water management

00:01:10.160 --> 00:01:14.720
Huge reservoirs, canals, channels and dikes
allowed them 3 harvests a year.

00:01:14.720 --> 00:01:16.740
It might have been the best technology of
the time.

00:01:16.740 --> 00:01:22.930
They built the temples at their peak. Khmer
empire ruler Jayavaraman VII, who also built

00:01:22.930 --> 00:01:26.030
Angkor Thom nearby, is the man credited.

00:01:26.030 --> 00:01:30.710
But a man's name doesn't really capture the
true size of the project.

00:01:30.710 --> 00:01:34.750
About 2 or 300 years later, the society collapsed.

00:01:34.750 --> 00:01:37.190
It wasn't all at once, or for one reason.

00:01:37.190 --> 00:01:42.540
But a factor archeologists and ecologists
hypothesized was that the incredible water

00:01:42.540 --> 00:01:43.160
management system failed

00:01:43.160 --> 00:01:47.490
due to a change in the climate -- dire drought,
then heavy floods.

00:01:47.490 --> 00:01:56.170
This possibility had been hypothesized before,
but a PHD student named Mary Beth Day spent

00:01:56.170 --> 00:01:57.500
6 years digging

00:01:57.500 --> 00:02:04.050
and found evidence for the theory in the form
of sediment records in the reservoir.

00:02:04.050 --> 00:02:13.170
What also is sure is that the ruling class
moved south.

00:02:13.170 --> 00:02:17.830
It's a beautiful place, but haunting: what
hundreds of thousands people would be living

00:02:17.830 --> 00:02:19.680
here had it survived?

00:02:19.680 --> 00:02:20.560
For around 400 or 500 years the forest merged
with Ta-prohm until it was 're-discovered'.

00:02:20.560 --> 00:02:24.820
It should give us pause. Our civilization
is a collaboration with nature.

00:02:24.820 --> 00:02:31.450
A giant structure must sway with the wind
and roll with the earthquakes, or else it

00:02:31.450 --> 00:02:33.370
will surely, quietly, fall.

00:02:33.370 --> 00:02:36.430
Hollywood films often depict the destruction
of urban centers.

00:02:36.430 --> 00:02:44.880
But the crisis comes at once, so we can band
together to fight it.

00:02:44.880 --> 00:02:49.620
In reality, giant cities have existed and
then died before. This one did.

00:02:49.620 --> 00:02:58.650
It just takes time -- the rain falls, the
roots grow, and nature eats what we built.

00:02:58.650 --> 00:03:01.170
The best technology of the time wasn't enough.

